<TABLE>
<TR>
<TD VALIGN=top>
<H2><FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.3.3.1.4.</FONT></H2>
</TD>
<TD VALIGN=top>
<H2>Normal Probability Plot: Data is Skewed Right</H2>
</TD>
</TR >
</TABLE>
</TD>
</TR>









<!-----graphic----->

<tr>
<td width=15% valign=top>
<I>
Normal Probability Plot for Data that is Skewed Right
</I>
</td>

<td width=85%>
<img src = "gif/normprp4.gif">
</td>
</tr>

<!-----conclusions----->

<tr>
<td width=15% valign=top>
<I>
Conclusions
</I>
</td>

<td width=85%>
<ol>
   <li>The normal probability plot shows a strongly non-linear
       pattern.  Specifically, it shows a quadratic pattern
       in which all the points are below a reference line drawn
       between the first and last points.
       </li>
   <li>The normal distribution is not a good model for this data.</li>
</ol>
</td>
</tr>

<!-----discussion----->

<tr>
<td width=15% valign=top>
<I>
Discussion
</I>
</td>

<td width=85%>
This quadratic pattern in the normal probability plot
is the signature of a significantly right skewed data set.
Likewise, if all the points on the normal probability plot fell
above the reference line connecting the first and last points, that
would be the signature pattern for a significantly left skewed data
set.
<p>
In this case, we can quite reasonably conclude that we need to
model this data with a right skewed distribution such as the
<a href="eda3668.htm">Weibull</a> or
<a href="eda3669.htm">lognormal</a>.

</td>
</tr>

<!-----end jjf----->
  
  
  
  
   

   
